SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 300 | Thursday, February 26, 2026

Good morning, and thank you for three hundred issues. We started this as a Monday email for forty people in a Slack group. No fanfare; on with the day.

1) Commerce opens a Section 232 review on aluminum extrusions
The department opened a review of whether the current aluminum tariff structure "adequately addresses" imported extrusions routed through third countries. Comments are due April 10. Window and door manufacturers are the exposed group; the extruders themselves are quietly pleased.

2) Laredo truck crossings set a February record
Northbound commercial crossings at the World Trade Bridge ran 8% above last February, the strongest month since the nearshoring wave began. Auto parts and appliances led. The bridge is at capacity from 10am to 2pm most weekdays; the new Laredo 4/5 bridge does not open until 2027.

3) Corvid Robotics raises $140M for pallet-handling robots
The Series C values the company at $1.1B. Their pitch is that the piece-picking robots everyone funded in 2021 solved the wrong problem: pallets and cases move most of the volume in a mid-size distribution center, and that work is where the labor shortage actually bites. We have seen two of their deployments and they are not wrong.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 22 weeks. Current lead time on 40-foot high-cube dry containers ordered from the two largest Chinese box makers, up from 9 weeks a year ago.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

